{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"1010add6-33cb-49bb-be25-d2ffe032db6f","status":"SUCCESS","response":"The Exokernel Approach lesson note (L02c) has been completely rewritten and brought to `solid` status.\n\nHere's a summary of the actions taken:\n- **Expanded Concepts:** Replaced all seed text with detailed explanations covering the Exokernel principle, Library Operating Systems, secure bindings, visible revocation, abort protocols, memory management, CPU scheduling, and downloaded packet filters. \n- **Vault Formatting:** Ensured that all text is formatted with exactly one full sentence per physical line in prose. Removed all em/en dashes (using `-` instead), avoided emojis, and used strict ASCII punctuation.\n- **Diagrams \u0026 Examples:** Included a Mermaid sequence diagram detailing how page fault handling and upcalls work, along with a worked numeric example comparing context switch costs for software vs. hardware TLB flushes.\n- **Comparison \u0026 Context:** Integrated a comparison table for Monolithic vs. Microkernel vs. Exokernel vs. SPIN architectures, and detailed the modern descendants (unikernels, eBPF, EPT). Added deep dives into the 4 source papers.\n- **Verification:** Ran `python3 tools/check_aos_coverage.py --lesson L02c --verbose` ensuring that all concept coverage rows produced no `seed`, `no heading`, or `under 250` errors.\n- **Git \u0026 Reporting:** Staged and committed the changes, and wrote the final report to `/Users/shreejitverma/github/career-ops/library/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/CS6210-AOS/crew/runner/reports/note-L02c.md`.\n","duration_seconds":131.311666,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":75389,"output_tokens":14920,"thinking_tokens":8892,"cache_read_tokens":442895,"total_tokens":90309}}}
